"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nprovide reasonable notice of license agreements that were located on a submerged screen); Hines\nv. Overstock, 668 F. Supp. 2d 362, 366 (E.D.N.Y. 2009) (ruling that plaintiff had no actual notice\nof the terms and conditions of use, the link to which was not visible without scrolling down to the\nbottom of the screen); Hoffman v. Supplements Togo Mgmt., LLC, 18 A.3d 210 (N.J. Super. Ct.\nApp. Div. 2011) (determining that a forum-selection clause was unreasonably masked from the\nview of prospective purchasers because of its circuitous mode of presentation, and was therefore\nunenforceable). But see Bernardino v. Barnes & Noble Booksellers, Inc., 2017 WL 7309893, at\n*11 (S.D.N.Y. Nov. 20, 2017) (holding that business's revisions to its checkout page, which\nincluded a prominent notice over the purchase button stating that \"[b]y making this purchase you\nare agreeing to our [terms of use],\" which were conspicuously hyperlinked, satisfied the\nconstructive-notice requirement); Maynez v. Walmart, Inc., 479 F. Supp. 3d 890 (C.D. Cal. 2020)\n(holding that \"the conspicuousness and placement of the 'Terms of Use' hyperlink\" on the screen\nand the requirement to affirmatively acknowledge and agree to such when placing an order\nprovided sufficient notice) (quoting Nguyen v. Barnes & Noble Inc., 763 F.3d 1171, 1177 (9th\nCir. 2014)); Bell v. Royal Seas Cruises, Inc., 2020 WL 5639947 (S.D. Fla. Sept. 21, 2020);\nMetroPCS Commc'ns, Inc. v. Porter, 273 So. 3d 1025, 1028 (Fla. Dist. Ct. App. 2018) (holding\nthat the browsewrap was enforceable because \"the hyperlink to the terms and conditions [was]\nconspicuous enough to put a reasonably prudent person on inquiry notice\"). Within the notice\nrequirement, courts also include an additional requirement that the terms be easily accessible. See,\ne.g., Hubbert v. Dell Corp., 835 N.E.2d 113, 122 (Ill. App. Ct. 2005) (\"The statement that the sales\nwere subject to the defendant's 'Terms and Conditions of Sale,' combined with making the 'Terms\nand Conditions of Sale' accessible online by blue hyperlinks, was sufficient notice to the plaintiffs\nthat purchasing the computers online would make the 'Terms and Conditions of Sale' binding on\nthem.\"); Van Tessell v. United Mktg. Grp., 795 F. Supp. 2d 770, 793 (N.D. Ill. 2011) (\"[T]he\nabsence of any reference to the Conditions of Use coupled with the multi-step process to locate\nthe Conditions of Use means that, like the plaintiffs in Specht, users of the ChefsCatalog.com\nwebsite could complete their purchases without ever having notice that their purchases are subject\nto the website's Conditions of Use.\"); Wilson v. Huuuge, Inc., 944 F.3d 1212, 1221 (9th Cir. 2019)\n(finding insufficient notice after explaining that \"[t]o find a reference, a user would need to click\non an ambiguous button to see the app's full profile page and scroll through multiple screen-lengths\nof similar-looking paragraphs. Once the user unearths the paragraph referencing the Terms, the\npage does not even inform the user that he will be bound by those terms\"); Hennessey v. Kohl's\nCorp., 571 F. Supp. 3d 1060 (E.D. Mo. 2021) (finding notice was insufficient because accessing\nthe terms required clicking on various hyperlinks to finally gain access to all legal notices, which\nwere inconspicuously placed). Courts evaluating the enforceability of browsewraps engage in a\nfact-specific, \"totality of the circumstances\" inquiry that focuses on the placement of the hyperlink\nand notices, as well as the general layout of the webpage. See Wilson, 944 F.3d at 1220 (holding\nthat constructive notice depends on \"the conspicuousness and placement of the terms and\nconditions, as well as the content and overall design of the app\"). This notice-and-opportunity-to-\nreview doctrine is restated in subsection (a).\n64\n@§2\n"